\title{Efficient Solar-Access Intervention Screening for Early Urban Renewal: An Auditable Decision-Support Workflow}
\author{}
\institute{}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
Architects considering solar-access improvements in an existing urban block need a manageable set of alternatives before investing in detailed design and simulation. This paper presents a bounded, auditable screening workflow: 630 rule-based two-building height-reduction candidates are ranked by declared preferences and an area--volume Pareto analysis; a curated set of 23 receives archived Honeybee/Radiance review; geometric translation and professional judgment remain separate gates. The case contains 45 building groups, of which 36 are eligible under research constraints. We report the actual top-ten shortlist with its performance, nominal volume costs and review status, rather than treating the candidates as completed architectural designs. A separately frozen 20-candidate same-site coverage cohort has low-sun-area mean absolute error of 0.45~m$^2$, resulting from one 9~m$^2$ grid-cell discrepancy. Historical matched tests support fast exhaustive screening: median full-universe runtime falls from 11,608.183 to 24.037~s through blocker reuse, including precomputation, for two specific single-thread implementations. Historical translation replays also expose outside-footprint walls; four contained corrections remove the observed adverse sunlight events. Current cloud work reanalyses archived aggregates and does not perform new site simulations. The contribution is an evidence-linked choice set for further architectural assessment, conditional on approximate canonical geometry, a fixed sampling protocol and explicit preferences. AI assistance in preparing this abstract is disclosed~\cite{openai2026}.
\keywords{Decision support \and Solar access \and Urban renewal \and Candidate screening \and Multi-criteria evaluation}
\end{abstract}

\section{Case, Decision Universe and Solar Setup}
\subsection{Bounded Candidate Universe}
Let $\mathcal{B}$ denote the 45 building groups, each represented by a footprint $F_i$ and height $h_i$.\aidraft{} The eligible subset $E\subset\mathcal{B}$ contains 36 groups selected using source flags for movable and protected buildings, strict Boolean parsing and $h_i>3$~m. A feasible rule decision satisfies
\begin{equation}
 z_i\in\{0,1\}\ (i\in E),\qquad \sum_{i\in E}z_i=2,
 \label{eq:select}
\end{equation}
with $z_i=0$ outside $E$. For a selected building, the rule height is
\begin{equation}
 h_i^r=\max\{3,\ h_i-\min(3,0.25h_i)\},\qquad
 h_i(z)=(1-z_i)h_i+z_i h_i^r, \label{eq:height}
\end{equation}
where heights are in metres. Thus $\binom{36}{2}=630$ unordered pairs constitute the complete declared universe. Exhaustive evaluation establishes an optimum only for a specified score within this finite universe. It says nothing about other numbers of altered buildings, alternative height rules, footprint changes, structural feasibility or legal rights.

For receiver $j$ and sample $t$, let $d_t=\ind\{\alpha_t>0\}$ indicate an above-horizon solar altitude and let $b_{jt}(z)$ indicate obstruction by at least one building. Direct-sun duration is
\begin{equation}
 D_j(z)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Delta t_t\,d_t\,[1-b_{jt}(z)].
 \label{eq:dsh}
\end{equation}
The geometric primitive intersects a horizontal ray with a footprint and uses the first positive projected distance $\ell$ to compare the ray height $0.1+\ell\tan\alpha_t$ against the building height. The source primitive ignores distances at most $10^{-7}$~m and uses a $10^{-9}$~m height tolerance. Point ordering, footprint construction, direction convention, tolerances and solar vectors are held identical in the matched comparison. The actual canonical case has no polygon holes; all receiving points were verified outside the canonical building footprints and off their boundaries. Those case-specific checks do not establish correctness for arbitrary polygon holes or boundary configurations.

\subsection{Metrics and Preferences}
Let $D_j^0$ be baseline duration, $P=2031$, $a=9$~m$^2$ the grid-area weight and $\tau=3$~h the research threshold. Four candidate metrics are
\begin{align}
 A(z)&=a\sum_j\big[\ind\{D_j^0<\tau\}-\ind\{D_j(z)<\tau\}\big],\label{eq:area}\\
 H(z)&=P^{-1}\sum_j[D_j(z)-D_j^0],\label{eq:hours}\\
 V(z)&=\sum_i |F_i|[h_i-h_i(z)],\label{eq:volume}\\
 N(z)&=a\sum_j\ind\{D_j^0\geq\tau,\ D_j(z)<\tau\}.\label{eq:new}
\end{align}
Here $A$ is net reduction of low-sun grid area, $H$ is mean duration change, $V$ is the nominal per-building volume proxy and $N$ is newly low-sun grid area. In a model with nonoverlapping fixed-footprint prisms, Eq.~\ref{eq:volume} measures removed volume. With the present overlapping footprints it is an additive proxy, not actual whole-scene net union-volume reduction or a construction quantity. Translated solids require a separate net union-volume calculation. The 3~h threshold defines this analysis; no statutory status is claimed.

For each $q\in\{A,H,V,N\}$, normalization bounds are fixed from the full 630-rule universe:
\begin{equation}
 n_q=\begin{cases}(q-q_{\min})/(q_{\max}-q_{\min}),&q_{\max}-q_{\min}\geq10^{-12},\\0,&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}
 \label{eq:norm}
\end{equation}
The preference score is
\begin{equation}
 S=0.45n_A+0.35n_H+0.15(1-n_V)+0.05(1-n_N).
 \label{eq:score}
\end{equation}
The weights are declared preferences, not learned parameters or probabilities. Bounds are not refitted to the review cohorts or Honeybee outcomes, and out-of-range values are not silently clipped. Table~\ref{tab:bounds} reports the frozen bounds. All rule candidates have $N=0$; hence the final term contributes a constant 0.05 and has no discriminating power in this universe. Changing only its weight cannot change the ordering. A historical sensitivity scenario that also changed the $A/H$ weights does not isolate the effect of $N$.

\section{Screening and Decision Workflow}
\subsection{From the Full Universe to a Reviewable Choice Set}
The workflow separates four decisions: what is eligible to be studied, which rule candidates merit review, whether a translated solid implements its rule, and whether an intervention is acceptable in practice.\aidraft{} The first three can be supported by explicit model records; the last requires professional and stakeholder judgment beyond the present data. Figure~\ref{fig:workflow} summarizes the progression without implying that every shortlisted candidate has a completed design translation.

For each building $i$, receiver $j$ and time $t$, precompute Boolean occlusion arrays $O_{ijt}$ and $R_{ijt}$ at the original and rule-reduced heights using the common primitive. Let $C_{jt}=\sum_i O_{ijt}$ be the original number of blockers. For selected pair $(a,b)$,
\begin{align}
 C_{jt}^{(a,b)}&=C_{jt}-O_{ajt}-O_{bjt}+R_{ajt}+R_{bjt},\label{eq:reuse}\\
 b_{jt}^{(a,b)}&=\ind\{C_{jt}^{(a,b)}>0\}.\label{eq:block}
\end{align}
These updates use a signed integer count type sufficiently wide for the building count. Subtraction in Boolean or unsigned storage can silently corrupt counts and is not an acceptable implementation of Eq.~\ref{eq:reuse}.

\section{Validation and Experimental Setup}
\subsection{Evidence Layers and Frozen Inputs}
Three historical evidence layers and the current aggregate reanalysis are kept separate.\aidraft{} The matched implementation experiment uses the canonical real-case input and the complete fixed candidate order. The same-metric agreement study recomputes statistics from frozen archived aggregates for a curated cohort of 23 and a separately preserved coverage cohort of 20. Finally, controlled Honeybee/Radiance replays revisit a baseline and five historical translations, followed by four corrected scenes. The original 20-candidate sample was neither reselected nor rerun in the correction experiment. The present cloud analysis reads archived candidate and validation tables, recomputes selection outputs and checks their consistency. Original site geometry, ordered coordinates and complete solver scenes are not available to this cloud analysis, so it performs no new site Honeybee/Radiance simulation. The historical timing and scene-replay measurements reported below retain their original execution context.

